# Error handling

Do not use empty `catch` blocks or catch without logging. Every `catch` must bind the error and log it.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — error is swallowed
try {
  await fetchData();
} catch {
  return fallback;
}

// ✅ GOOD — log, then recover or rethrow
try {
  await fetchData();
} catch (error) {
  console.warn('Fetch failed, using fallback:', error);
  return fallback;
}
```

- Use `console.error` when the failure is unexpected or user-facing.
- Use `console.warn` when falling back to an intentional alternate path (offline cache, precached shell, etc.).
- Re-throw when the caller should handle the failure.
